### III. PHP SUB-MODULE ARCHITECTURE & FILE BREAKDOWN

Monolithic PHP files must be systematically broken down into distinct sub-modules housed within `includes/[module_name]/`. Each sub-module file must maintain complete isolation:

1. `class-sywat-[module].php` (Controller Shell & Entry Point):
   - Handles sub-module inclusions (`require_once`), shortcodes (`add_shortcode`), capability/permission checks (`sywat_can_access_*`), asset enqueueing (Section IV.1), and execution orchestration.
   - Every NEW shortcode tag MUST also be registered in `sywat_shortcode_tags()` (via the `sywat_shortcode_tags` filter or the list in `class-sywat-frontend.php`). Registration is what gives the page plugin assets, the `.sywat-scope` wrapper, the page-builder placeholder, and no-cache headers.

2. MANDATORY DATA & HELPER FILE SEPARATION:
   - `[module]-data.php` (Data Access & Metric Engines): Pure database queries (`WP_User_Query`, `$wpdb` calls), user meta getters, raw dataset aggregations, and statistical calculation engines. Must NEVER render HTML or format presentation strings.
   - `[module]-helpers.php` (Formatting & Presentation Helpers): Pure scalar/array payload formatters, HTML/badge label generators, array sorting weight maps, and DOB/age division calculators. Must NEVER execute database queries or fetch meta directly.

3. `[module]-actions.php`:
   - Pure POST request processing, nonce verification, data sanitization, AJAX endpoint hooks, and database mutations. Returns standardized alert markup (`$action_notice`) or JSON responses.
   - Any handler that returns JSON (or redirects, or sets cookies) MUST run BEFORE the theme starts output: register it on `wp_ajax_*` / `wp_ajax_nopriv_*` or on `template_redirect` (with a capability check). NEVER call `wp_send_json_*()`, `wp_redirect()` or `setcookie()` from inside a shortcode render function: by then the theme has already printed the page header and the response is corrupted (root cause of the 6.7.0 score auto-save failure).

4. `[module]-views.php` & `[module]-modals.php`:
   - `[module]-views.php`: Visual HTML renderers for primary UI structures (tabs, master cards, filter bars, tables).
   - `[module]-modals.php`: Pop-up overlay templates. Must receive pre-calculated data payload arrays and format output via helpers. Must follow the Modal Markup Contract (Section V.3).

5. NO OUTPUT AT FILE-INCLUDE TIME:
   - PHP files may ONLY define functions, classes, constants and hooks at the top level. NEVER emit HTML, `<script>`, whitespace or a closing `?>` followed by content at file scope. Output at include time is printed on every request (pages, AJAX, REST, feeds) ahead of `<!DOCTYPE html>` and forces browsers into quirks mode (root cause of the 6.7.0 stray-script bug in `checkin-views.php`).
   - Omit the closing `?>` at the end of pure-PHP files.

6. INTERNAL LINKS & REDIRECTS:
   - Links between plugin screens MUST use `sywat_page_url( '<key>' )` (optionally with `add_query_arg()`). NEVER hard-code paths (`'/sywat-desk/'`, `href="/..."`) or call `site_url( '/slug/' )`. New screens get a new key in `sywat_page_defaults()` so the path appears under Settings > SYWAT Display.
   - User-supplied redirect targets MUST pass through `wp_validate_redirect()`.

7. BOARD POSITIONS & AUTOMATED ROLE SYNCHRONIZATION POLICY:
   - Board Position assignments saved under Board Management (`save_board_member`) serve as the single source of truth for Officer and Governor roles (`tfaa_president`, `tfaa_field_governor`, etc.).
   - Board position saves MUST automatically add matching WP user roles and revoke unassigned officer roles.
   - General profile updates (`update_member`) MUST preserve active board officer roles so profile saves never strip assigned board status.

8. FILE & PATH SAFETY:
   - Never trust a file path, URL or filename sent back from the browser. Staged files must be validated against their storage folder (pattern: `sywat_validate_import_path()` restricting to `uploads/sywat-temp/`).
   - Never ship data files (spreadsheets, exports, member lists, prompts) inside the plugin folder. Everything in `wp-content/plugins/` is publicly downloadable.

