### V. STRICT CSS, HTML COMPONENT & UI POLICIES

1. ABSOLUTE PROHIBITION OF INLINE STYLES & EMBEDDED CSS:
   - NEVER put `<style>...</style>` tags inside PHP templates or functions.
   - NEVER use inline `style="..."` attributes on HTML elements in PHP views, forms, or modal templates. Use centralized CSS utility classes (`.sywat-mt-3`, `.sywat-form-grid-2`, `.sywat-hidden`). (Sole exception: the printable invoice inside `class-sywat-accounting.php`, pending refactor.)

2. DESIGN TOKEN & COLOR PALETTE BINDING (THEMEABLE):
   - Brand colours and fonts MUST be referenced through tokens, NEVER as hex values in component rules. The TFAA Divi child theme restyles the plugin by defining `--tfaa-*` variables; a hard-coded hex silently escapes that theming.
   - Token map (defined in `sywat-core.css` as `--sywat-X: var(--tfaa-X, <original hex>)`):
     - Brand: `--sywat-navy`, `--sywat-navy-soft`, `--sywat-primary`, `--sywat-primary-dark`, `--sywat-primary-tint`, `--sywat-accent`, `--sywat-heading`.
     - Text: `--sywat-text`, `--sywat-text-muted`, `--sywat-text-subtle`.
     - Borders: `--sywat-border`, `--sywat-border-light`, `--sywat-border-strong`.
     - Surfaces: `--sywat-surface-alt`, `--sywat-surface-muted`.
     - Font: `--sywat-font-body`.
   - Primary action triggers (submits, save buttons, sign-in buttons) MUST use `.sywat-btn-primary`, which renders `var(--sywat-primary)` with `var(--sywat-primary-dark)` on hover. Default SYWAT Action Blue is `#0284c7` / `#0369a1`; TFAA red is supplied by the theme.
   - Secondary triggers map to `.sywat-btn-secondary`, destructive triggers to `.sywat-btn-danger`, confirmation states to `.sywat-btn-success`.
   - Status colours (success green, danger red, warning amber) stay fixed and are used only through the existing status classes (`.sywat-btn-success`, `.sywat-btn-danger`, `.sywat-alert-*`, `.sywat-badge-*`).
   - A genuinely new brand colour requires a new token in the `:root` block (`--sywat-new: var(--tfaa-new, #hex);`) and a note in README.md. Never inline the hex.
   - Translucent variants use `color-mix(in srgb, var(--sywat-primary) 20%, transparent)`, preceded by an `rgba()` fallback declaration.

3. STRICT MODAL CONTROL COMPONENT MANDATE & MARKUP CONTRACT:
   - Every modal MUST use this exact structure, which `assets/js/sywat-modal-manager.js` depends on. The manager moves top-level modals to `<body>` (escaping Divi transforms and fixed headers) and adds dialog roles, focus handling, Escape-to-close, scroll lock and print isolation:
     ```
     div.sywat-modal-backdrop#sywat_<name>_modal          (open = add class "active")
       div.sywat-modal-content [.sywat-modal-sm|md|lg|xl]
         div.sywat-modal-header
           h3  (dialog title, becomes aria-labelledby)
           button.sywat-modal-close (type="button" or type="submit")
         div.sywat-modal-body
         div.sywat-modal-footer
     ```
   - A `<form>` goes INSIDE `.sywat-modal-content` (or the body). A modal must NEVER be placed inside a `<form>`: the manager cannot move it, and it stays trapped inside the theme section.
   - Open and close ONLY by toggling the `active` class (or `sywat-modal-active`). NEVER toggle `style.display`, never set z-index, never set `position: fixed` on any other plugin element.
   - Stacking uses the variables `--sywat-z-modal`, `--sywat-z-modal-nested`, `--sywat-z-modal-dropdown` and `--sywat-z-modal-alert` only. Hard-coded z-index values (99999 etc.) are prohibited.
   - Non-dismissible modals (progress, blocking alerts) carry `data-sywat-static="true"` so Escape does not close them.
   - Modal close buttons (`.sywat-modal-close`) and modal footer action buttons MUST strictly use native `<button type="button">` or `<button type="submit">` elements. Icon-only buttons MUST carry `aria-label`.
   - NEVER convert modal buttons or close triggers into `<a>` anchor links to work around JavaScript state handling or URL parameter cleanup bugs.
   - All URL parameter removal (e.g., clearing `edit_board_user_id`) MUST be executed cleanly via `window.history.replaceState()` inside the module's `assets/js/` file.

4. STRICT SEPARATION OF COSMETIC VS. STRUCTURAL EDITS:
   - Cosmetic, spacing, label positioning, and alignment fixes MUST be executed exclusively within CSS files (`assets/css/`).
   - NEVER modify PHP view files or template wrappers to solve a visual styling bug unless an RCA explicitly proves a missing CSS class or DOM element is required.

5. SINGLE-SELECTOR SCOPE ISOLATION:
   - CSS edits must strictly target the specific component or class requiring adjustment.
   - NEVER alter parent layout wrappers, default container flex behaviors, or global rules when fixing an isolated child element alignment.

6. THEME-PROOF SELECTOR RULES:
   - Every selector MUST start with a `.sywat-` class (or `#sywat_` id). NEVER write bare element selectors (`body`, `h2`, `table`, `td`, `input`, `button`, `a`, `header`, `footer`); they restyle the whole site.
   - NEVER target theme markup: `.entry-content`, `.entry-title`, `#main-header`, `#page-container`, `.et_pb_*`, `.et-l--*`, `.ast-*`, `#masthead`, `.site-header`, etc. (Sole exception: the App-mode header rules already in `sywat-core.css`, gated by `body.sywat-hide-theme-chrome`.)
   - Element rules inside plugin UI (form fields, table cells, links) MUST use the shared scope prefix `:is(.sywat-wrap, .sywat-app, .sywat-scope, .sywat-modal-backdrop) <element>`. This beats theme content rules (e.g., Divi's `.entry-content tr td`) and keeps applying after modals are moved to `<body>`. Never use `.sywat-wrap X` alone.
   - Do not use `!important` in new rules. Win with correct scoping/specificity instead. (Legacy `!important` stays until refactored; never add more to "beat" another `!important`. Fix the scope.)
   - Typography inherits `var(--sywat-font-body)`. Do not set `font-family` elsewhere (monospace for codes excepted).
   - Every new form field type used in a module must be covered by the scoped input rule in `sywat-core.css`. Unstyled fields fall back to the theme's look.

7. LAYOUT & RESPONSIVENESS:
   - Prefer fluid layouts that size to their container (`flex-wrap: wrap`, `grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(…, 1fr))`, `min()`, `%`) over fixed pixel widths. In Theme mode the plugin sits inside narrower Divi rows.
   - When a media query is required, reuse the plugin's existing breakpoints (992px, 768px, 600px). Do not invent new breakpoints. (Do NOT switch to Divi's 980/767/479 piecemeal; that is a planned, site-wide design decision.)
   - Data tables MUST sit inside `.sywat-table-responsive` (horizontal scroll) and use `.sywat-table`. On phones, wide list tables should offer a stacked/card layout or a sticky first (name) column.
   - Every screen and modal must work at 390px width with no horizontal page scroll.

8. PRINT:
   - Printable output (rosters, invoices) MUST be rendered inside an open modal or a `#sywat_print_area` container. The global print rules then hide everything else, whatever the theme.
   - Mark non-printing controls with `.no-print`. Never add theme-specific selectors to print CSS.

9. ICONS & ACCESSIBILITY:
   - New UI must not rely on emoji as icons (rendering varies by device and screen readers read them aloud). Use inline SVG with `aria-hidden="true"` next to a text label, or a CSS mask icon. Existing emoji remain until the design pass.
   - Use real `<button>`, `<a href>`, `<label for>` + inputs. Never put click handlers on `<div>`/`<span>`. Never use `href="#"` + `onclick` for actions (themes with smooth-scroll hijack `#` links); use a `<button type="button">`.
   - Text contrast 4.5:1 minimum, visible keyboard focus (do not remove `:focus-visible` outlines).

